WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:04.020
In this video,

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:08.660
we will look at an extremely important concept related to polynomials.

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:09.920
Think about it.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:14.180
They can actually be just two broad categories of polynomials.

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:17.740
The first category which has just one variable.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.560
And the second one which has more than one variable.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:25.500
That's it, these are the only two possibilities.

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:27.580
An example of the first type

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:34.260
would be '6x raised to 5' plus '2x squared minus 7x' plus '9'.

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:38.980
This polynomial has just one variable, which is 'x'.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:41.600
An example of the second type would be

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:48.200
'5xy cubed' plus '17xy' plus '9y squared' plus '9.'

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:50.300
This has two variables,

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:54.140
'x' and 'y' which makes it fall into the second category.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:57.060
So what about these two categories?

00:00:57.380 --> 00:00:59.520
Why did we form them?

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:02.940
It is for the concept we will look at in this video.

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:05.760
The degree of the polynomial.

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:07.340
In the first category,

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:09.440
where we have just one variable,

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:13.540
the degree is the largest exponent of that variable.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:16.760
Here 5 is the largest exponent,

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.860
which tells us that the degree is 5.

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:23.520
The degree of this polynomial is 5.

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:27.000
Let's look at a second example in this category.

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:32.660
'21x' plus '6x cubed' plus '7' plus 'x squared.'

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:36.240
In the first term the degree of 'x' is 1.

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:40.000
In the second term, the degree of 'x' is 3.

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:42.800
And in the last term it's 2 .

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:46.500
3 becomes the largest exponent of the variable.

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:50.900
And that's why the degree of this polynomial is 3.

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:53.780
Is it any different in the second case?

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:56.100
Yes, slightly different.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:57.780
In the second case,

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:02.960
for each term we add the exponents of each variable in it.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:05.660
How will it work out for the first example?

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:09.760
Remember, we have to look at each term individually.

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:13.380
Consider the first term, '5xy cubed.'

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:18.280
We had to add the exponents of each variable in it.

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:23.740
The exponent of 'x' is 1 and the exponent of 'y' is 3.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:25.420
So for the first term,

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:29.820
we consider the power to be '1 plus 3 which is 4.'

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.100
Now we come to the second term.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:33.900
There are two variables,

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:39.040
'x' and 'y' and each of them have an exponent equal to 1.

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:43.460
Adding the exponents, we consider the exponent of this term

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:46.980
to be '1 plus 1 which equals 2.'

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:50.400
In the third term, we have just one variable.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:53.400
Its exponent will just be 2.

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:56.640
And as we have no variables in the last term,

00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:59.360
its exponent will be 0.

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:03.180
Remember, we are looking at exponents of variables.

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:07.360
The exponent of variables of the last term is 0.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:10.860
And the largest of these becomes the degree.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:13.600
The degree of this polynomial is 4

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:16.160
which is the highest amongst these.

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:18.840
Here's our last example.

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:26.340
'7xz' plus '10' plus '3z cubed y' plus '19y raised to 4.'

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:28.820
We have four terms here.

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:33.740
In the first term, the sum of exponents of 'x' and 'z' is 2.

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:37.020
In the second case, since we have no variables,

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:39.380
the exponent is 0.

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:43.140
In the third term, it's '3 plus 1 which equals 4.'

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:47.440
And in the fourth term as well the exponent is 4.

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:50.980
4 is the highest amongst these four terms.

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:55.720
Hence the degree of this polynomial is 4.

